      "classes": "1: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a bank.\n2: NO MATCH -- An operator is not an IT operations team.\n3: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a service desk.\n4: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a network operations centre.\n5: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a security operations centre.\n6: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes an incident response team.\n7: NO MATCH -- An authorised operator is not necessarily an on-call engineer.\n8: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a service owner.\n9: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes an application owner.\n10: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a technical owner.\n11: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a business owner.\n12: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a vendor.\n13: NO MATCH -- Stakeholder communication is not a stakeholder.\n14: NO MATCH -- A critical business transaction is not a business process.\n15: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a service level objective.\n16: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes an application.\n17: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a database.\n18: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes an infrastructure component.\n19: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a server.\n20: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a virtual machine.\n21: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a container platform.\n22: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a cloud service.\n23: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a network device.\n24: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a network segment.\n25: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes an endpoint.\n26: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a storage system.\n27: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a data centre.\n28: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a site.\n29: NO MATCH -- A recovery environment is a specific kind of environment, not the general class.\n30: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a data asset.\n31: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes an integration interface.\n32: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a third-party service.\n33: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a monitoring tool.\n34: NO MATCH -- A verification check is not a metric.\n35: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a log source.\n36: NO MATCH -- A containment action is not a security event.\n37: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a major incident.\n38: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a cybersecurity incident.\n39: NO MATCH -- A containment action is not an emergency change.\n40: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a release.\n41: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a deployment.\n42: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a runbook.\n43: NO MATCH -- No candidate denotes a business continuity plan.\n44: NO MATCH -- A verification check is not a disaster recovery test.\n45: MATCH 5 -- Stakeholder communication is an incident communication.\n46: MATCH 7 -- The candidate is a regulatory notification concerning an incident.",
      "relationships": "1: NO MATCH -- No candidate connects Business Service to Customer Channel through delivery.\n2: MATCH 4 -- An IT service supporting a business service is the reciprocal of that business service depending on it.\n3: MATCH 17 -- Both say a backup set protects a configuration item.\n4: NO MATCH -- Applying to an IT service does not necessarily mean recovering it.\n5: NO MATCH -- No candidate says a recovery plan uses a backup set.\n6: NO MATCH -- No candidate says an event is observed on a configuration item.\n7: NO MATCH -- No candidate relates an event to an IT service.\n8: MATCH 9 -- An alert based on a monitoring rule corresponds to one generated by that rule.\n9: NO MATCH -- Being triggered by an event is not the same relationship as indicating it.\n10: NO MATCH -- Correlating an alert into an incident is not opening one.\n11: MATCH 2 -- Both describe events being correlated into an incident.\n12: NO MATCH -- No candidate says an incident impacts a business service.\n13: NO MATCH -- Concerning a configuration item does not necessarily mean affecting it.\n14: MATCH 10 -- An incident having evidence corresponds to its being supported by evidence items.\n15: NO MATCH -- A change being for an incident does not mean it resolves the incident.\n16: MATCH 20 -- Using a recovery plan matches being recovered with that plan.\n17: MATCH 11 -- An incident's materiality assessment evaluates that incident.\n18: MATCH 12 -- An incident's post-incident review reviews that incident.\n19: MATCH 18 -- Both say a known error documents a problem.\n20: NO MATCH -- No candidate says a known error affects a configuration item.\n21: NO MATCH -- Affecting a configuration item does not necessarily mean modifying it.\n22: NO MATCH -- No candidate says a change affects an IT service.\n23: MATCH 19 -- Both say a change implements a corrective action.",
      "properties": "1: NO MATCH -- None identifies the incident summary.\n2: NO MATCH -- None provides the incident description.\n3: NO MATCH -- None records when the incident was detected.\n4: NO MATCH -- None records when the incident was opened.\n5: NO MATCH -- None records when the incident was resolved.\n6: NO MATCH -- None records when the incident was closed.\n7: NO MATCH -- Major-incident declaration is not incident priority.\n8: NO MATCH -- None represents incident urgency.\n9: NO MATCH -- A cybersecurity flag is not the incident category.\n10: NO MATCH -- None identifies the detection source.\n11: NO MATCH -- None indicates whether the incident recurs.\n12: NO MATCH -- Communication cadence is not time to acknowledge.\n13: NO MATCH -- Recovery-point acceptability is not time to restore.\n14: NO MATCH -- Change kind is not a summary.\n15: NO MATCH -- Implementation verification is not overall change status.\n16: NO MATCH -- Neither candidate records the planned start.\n17: NO MATCH -- Neither candidate records the planned end.\n18: NO MATCH -- Evidence kind is not its collection time.\n19: NO MATCH -- Evidence kind is not its integrity status.\n20: NO MATCH -- Evidence kind is not its cryptographic hash.\n21: NO MATCH -- Review status is not the date it was held.\n22: NO MATCH -- Review status is not its findings.",
